{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And I will give peace in the land.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say this after He had already said ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם לָבֶטַח״. Perhaps it refers to the people of Israel themselves, that there be no division of hearts among them, that HaShem may plant peace and friendship between them. Or it may refer to the totality of the world — and you should know that when Scripture speaks of the land of Israel it mentions it with a definite designation, as it says nearby ״בְּאַרְצְכֶם״, whereas here He says <b>in the land</b>, thereby promising them that there will be peace in the entire world. And you will find that HaShem desires this matter; go out and learn from the festival sacrifices — seventy corresponding to seventy nations — and from the statements of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sukkah 55b) concerning this matter. Also according to nature, when there are wars in the world, even those who dwell securely tremble at the sound of the tumult of war, and therefore He concluded: <b>and you shall lie down and none shall terrify</b>." "<b>And I will cause evil beasts to cease.</b> Meaning: since HaShem promised them that they would dwell securely, each man under his vine and under his fig tree, from this it would follow that the roads somewhat distant from the settlement would see little foot traffic, and since this is so, desert beasts would be found there and would draw near to come into the settled area. Therefore He said <b>and I will cause evil beasts to cease from the land</b> — meaning, the land that is liable to have an evil beast in it, which is the place near the settlement. And for this reason He did not say 'in your land,' as He had begun to say nearby both before it and after it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:42.869402218Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}